            <ArticleTitle>A diaphragmatic retroperitoneal cyst.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>e77-8</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1308/003588415X14181254790329</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Diaphragmatic lesions are usually congenital bronchogenic cysts. A patient with a known diaphragmatic cyst presented with new onset right upper quadrant pain. Repeat imaging showed enlargement of the cyst, the CA19-9 cancer marker was raised at 312 iu/ml (normal: &lt;27 iu/ml) and positron emission tomography combined with computed tomography showed focally increased uptake in the cystic wall. In view of symptoms and risk of neoplasia, the lesion was excised. Histology showed a benign epidermoid cyst. Features falsely suggesting neoplasia have been reported previously with benign splenic cysts but not with a benign diaphragmatic epidermoid cyst.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
